      "spec": "Create 8 D flip-flops with an active-high asynchronous reset. Reset sets the output to 0. All flip-flops are triggered by the positive edge of clk.",
      "v0": {
        "version": 0,
        "official_pass": false,
        "code": "module TopModule (\n  input clk,\n  input [7:0] d,\n  input areset,\n  output reg [7:0] q\n);\n\n  // reset is sampled on the clock edge\n  always @(posedge clk) begin\n    if (areset)\n      q <= 8'b0;\n    else\n      q <= d;\n  end\n\nendmodule\n"
      },
      "vbest": {
        "version": 4,
        "official_pass": true,
        "code": "module TopModule (\n  input clk,\n  input [7:0] d,\n  input areset,\n  output reg [7:0] q\n);\n\n  // asynchronous reset: areset is in the sensitivity list\n  always @(posedge clk, posedge areset) begin\n    if (areset)\n      q <= 8'b0;\n    else\n      q <= d;\n  end\n\nendmodule\n"
      },
      "explanation": {
        "round": 4,
        "summary": "Put asynchronous resets in the sensitivity list: always @(posedge clk, posedge areset)",
        "reasoning": "Version 0 only looked at areset on a rising clock edge, so whenever reset rose between edges q kept its old value for half a cycle. In round 4 the Builder coach saw the Breaker exploit this on 4 designs and added the rule: \"asynchronous\" in the spec means the reset goes in the sensitivity list."
      },
